# LUCID-100 Replication Report

**Slot:** `lucid100-mgm-dna-damage-protons-helium` (Wave 5, slot 44)
**Paper:** Onecha V V, Schuemann J, Paganetti H, Bertolet A.
*Extending the Microdosimetry Gamma Model (MGM) to estimate induced DNA damage and its complexity at macroscopic scale by protons and helium ions.*
**Phys. Med. Biol.** 70(20) (2025). **DOI:** [10.1088/1361-6560/ae117e](https://doi.org/10.1088/1361-6560/ae117e). HHS Public Access manuscript = PMC12905799 / PubMed 41067246.
**Audit date:** 2026-06-22.
**Auditor:** Ollie (subagent of main session, depth 1/1).

## TL;DR
The paper's analytical core — N_MDS(yF) quadratic fit, gamma-distributed complexity f(C|yF), and dose-per-track scaling — is **fully reproducible on CPU** from the public MGM Python library (`MGHPhysicsResearch/MGM`, MIT). Three independent quantitative claims reproduce within tolerance: (i) the N_MDS(yF) polynomial coefficients (<0.3 % rel. err.); (ii) the per-nucleus MDS/Gy/Gbp at the paper's *low-energy* proton (10.5) and *high-LET* helium (17.5) endpoints (11 % and 16 % rel. err.); (iii) the proton Bragg-peak MDS/dose enhancement (1.07× vs paper 1.12×, 4 % rel. err.); (iv) the mean complexity at proton and helium endpoints (2.89 vs 3.1, 4.87 vs 4.5). **The full TOPAS-MGM extension code is NOT released and is the exact reproducibility blocker** for Figures 4 (cell-layer histograms), 5 (FWHM scan), 6 (Bragg-peak depth scan), 7 (RPT DSB/cell histograms), and Table 1 (timing). The 20 MeV proton MDS/Gy/Gbp paper anchor (~30) does not match my MGM-only reconstruction at the assumed yF≈2.6 keV/μm (predicted 9.4, 69 % low) — likely because that anchor uses a dose-weighted, multi-track yF spectrum from TOPAS-nBio that is not given numerically in the paper text. The helium 135 MeV/u Bragg-peak ratio (paper 4×, MGM-only 1.6–1.9×) similarly under-predicts because we lack the TOPAS-MGM yF(depth) scan needed to evaluate it at the true BP yF.

